Title:
Bypassing EDR with Local AI

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explores using locally run/open-weight and “uncensored” LLMs to rapidly generate and iteratively refine a Windows LSASS dumping tool that evades modern EDR detections. The author benchmarks whether an AI can produce an executable that dumps LSASS with minimal human guidance, highlighting that mainstream hosted models (e.g., Claude) refuse, while open models (DeepSeek) will generate functional code but may still be detected. By switching to an uncensored Qwen 27B model run on local GPUs, the AI suggests and implements stealth-oriented changes that result in no detections across two lab EDR products. The techniques include process cloning/suspended process handling, in-memory minidump creation, XOR “encryption,” and operational tweaks to reduce suspicious indicators. It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters developing bespoke post-exploitation tooling, and it’s important for defenders because it demonstrates how cheap local compute can accelerate EDR evasion iteration.

Technical Focus:
- LSASS credential dumping and minidump generation
- EDR evasion via behavioral/telemetry reduction
- Process access rights minimization (reduced access masks)
- Suspended process / process cloning and reflection-based techniques
- String/indicator scrubbing and output/path randomization
- Local/offline LLM use for offensive tool development

Use Cases:
- Rapid prototyping of custom LSASS dumpers for post-exploitation
- Iterating on tooling to reduce EDR detections in lab simulations
- Generating variations of tradecraft (sleep jitter, string obfuscation, access mask tuning)
- Red team research into how model guardrails affect offensive capability
- Blue team threat modeling of AI-assisted malware/tooling development

Keywords:
LSASS, credential dumping, minidump, pypykatz, EDR evasion, Windows internals, process access mask, suspended process, process cloning, in-memory dumping, XOR encryption, string obfuscation, sleep jitter, telemetry reduction, local LLM, open-weight models, DeepSeek, Qwen 27B, guardrails, post-exploitation